WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.320
Red and green image channels overlap.

2
00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:05.940
Does the yellow patch mean the two proteins bind?

3
00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:10.800
Chapter one showed why close objects merge into one spot.

4
00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:14.440
Now follow the evidence from label to claim.

5
00:00:17.933 --> 00:00:19.913
Start with the blur from chapter one.

6
00:00:21.493 --> 00:00:25.153
Across the image, it is about 230 nanometres wide.

7
00:00:26.453 --> 00:00:30.433
Along the depth direction, it stretches to about 800 nanometres.

8
00:00:31.793 --> 00:00:36.293
A yellow pixel alone cannot show that the two sources are closer than this scale.

9
00:00:37.383 --> 00:00:40.803
These dimensions use chapter one's green-light settings.

10
00:00:42.143 --> 00:00:46.363
A red channel usually has a longer emission wavelength and a larger blur.

11
00:00:47.703 --> 00:00:51.463
For a two-colour claim, use the measured resolution of each channel.

12
00:00:52.517 --> 00:00:54.157
Here is a question to hold.

13
00:00:55.437 --> 00:01:00.377
At typical cellular protein density, how many protein molecules would this volume

14
00:01:00.377 --> 00:01:04.997
contain? A handful, a few hundred, or tens of thousands?

15
00:01:11.083 --> 00:01:12.963
Treat the blur as an ellipsoid.

16
00:01:14.143 --> 00:01:17.443
Its volume is roughly 0.02 cubic micrometres.

17
00:01:18.763 --> 00:01:23.643
At typical cellular protein density, it contains tens of thousands of protein

18
00:01:23.643 --> 00:01:24.643
molecules.

19
00:01:25.317 --> 00:01:30.997
So, after the controls, an in-focus overlap can support proximity within about this

20
00:01:30.997 --> 00:01:34.517
volume. It does not say the two proteins touch.

21
00:01:35.277 --> 00:01:40.757
In widefield, out-of-focus haze can create apparent overlap from farther away.

22
00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:44.230
Even that weaker claim needs evidence.

23
00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:48.010
Colour overlap is a claim about proximity.

24
00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:52.210
It is only as strong as each link that produced it.

25
00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:54.530
Check three links in turn.

26
00:01:56.483 --> 00:02:01.303
First, the label. An antibody can bind the wrong protein.

27
00:02:02.943 --> 00:02:05.463
Signal loss after knockout supports specificity.

28
00:02:06.823 --> 00:02:10.703
With knockdown, the signal should fall with verified target reduction.

29
00:02:10.703 --> 00:02:16.943
A secondary-only sample checks nonspecific signal from the fluorescent secondary.

30
00:02:18.067 --> 00:02:19.327
Second, the channels.

31
00:02:20.547 --> 00:02:23.267
A dye's light can leak into another colour channel.

32
00:02:24.527 --> 00:02:26.717
That leak is called bleed-through.

33
00:02:26.717 --> 00:02:31.807
Single-label samples measure it, and multicolour beads test whether the channels

34
00:02:31.807 --> 00:02:38.087
line up. Misregistration can separate matching signals or superimpose different

35
00:02:38.087 --> 00:02:39.087
ones.

36
00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:40.650
Third, the record.

37
00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:44.810
Image every condition with identical settings.

38
00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:49.190
Keep the relevant pixels below the detector maximum.

39
00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:55.130
Background subtraction removes the average background, but not its photon noise.

40
00:02:56.233 --> 00:02:57.713
Now return to yellow.

41
00:02:59.013 --> 00:03:04.113
If every link holds, an in-focus overlap supports proximity at roughly one

42
00:03:04.113 --> 00:03:05.173
resolution volume.

43
00:03:06.873 --> 00:03:09.833
Binding is a separate claim, and it needs other experiments.

44
00:03:11.233 --> 00:03:15.853
Co-immunoprecipitation supports membership in the same complex, but not direct

45
00:03:15.853 --> 00:03:21.533
contact. A test with purified proteins addresses direct interaction.

46
00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:26.160
So read a colour overlap the way chapter one read the blur.

47
00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:31.860
Ask what was labelled, what leaked between channels, and what the detector recorded.

48
00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:36.980
The overlap reports what the measurement could separate, and nothing finer.

49
00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:40.720
Overlap supports proximity, not binding.
